From: the SK-D840N 7.3 line
Subject: [PATCH] net: zx279133: the 2.5G jack's transport VID has to be in the tree's range

The 2.5G jack is the 9132's fifth port - the vendor's outerphy, PPU 5 -
and it is DSA port 8, so it carries the transport VID 55 + 8 = 63.  The
board layer already widens the tagger's own range to that port
(ZX279133_RTL8372N_USER_PORT_MAX 8 in
include/net/dsa_zx279133_rtl8372n.h), so a frame that comes in on the 2.5G
jack arrives tagged with 63 and is dispatched to the right port.  The
receive side of the jack works.

This tree's own copy of the range stops at 62, and the transmit side reads
it.  The DSA tagger writes the transport VID of the port a frame is for
onto the frame as it leaves for a jack - the 2.5G jack's frame leaves
with a transport VID of 63 - and the LAN egress
(zx279133_lan_strip_transport_vlan()) only recognises the VIDs inside the
tree's range.  A VID of 63 is outside it, so the egress neither takes the
tag off the frame nor reads the chip port out of it: the frame leaves the
chip aimed at the fallback port (2, the second UNI jack) with the tag
still on it, and the client on the 2.5G jack sees nothing.  That is the
whole of the egress failure - the board counts the frame as sent, and no
frame comes out of the jack.

So the range has to include the fifth port: 59..63, which is what the 6.18
generation has and what its 2.5G jack works with
(docs/UPSTREAM-2G5-LANE-FIX section 12.7).

The table of the logical LAN ports' MF extension entries is indexed by the
transport VID, and the logical port 63 - the fifth one - needs an entry of
its own: without it the table is read one entry past its end and whatever
follows it in memory is written into the switch's table.  The entry is the
one number in that table that is not a measurement: the factory system has
four LAN ports and no fifth, so there is nothing on this board to read the
fifth entry from.  What the four measured entries do is hand out the
per-port areas in a run - word 1 steps by 0x00a00a00 and word 2 by 0x28
from one port to the next, and word 0, the port attribute word, is the
same for all four - and this entry continues both runs.  It is the same
entry, word for word, that the 6.18 generation carries.

The static assertion is the 6.18 generation's: the table is indexed by the
transport VID, so a logical port with no entry reads past the end of the
array.

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133.h
@@ -53,7 +53,24 @@
 #define ZX279133_LAN_TX_WORD4		0x003e0000
 #define ZX279133_LAN_RX_QUEUE_COUNT	8
 #define ZX279133_LAN_TRANSPORT_VID_MIN	59
-#define ZX279133_LAN_TRANSPORT_VID_MAX	62
+/*
+ * The largest transport VID the LAN side uses.
+ *
+ * The 9132 has five jacks: the four UNI ports carry the transport VIDs
+ * 59..62, and the 2.5G jack is the fifth one - the vendor's outerphy,
+ * PPU 5 - which is DSA port 8 and carries the transport VID 55 + 8 = 63.
+ * The tagger on this board already accepts that VID; this range is what
+ * the transmit path reads.
+ *
+ * It stopped at 62, and that is what the egress failure was: a frame the
+ * CPU sends to the 2.5G jack leaves the tagger with a transport VID of 63,
+ * the LAN egress does not recognise the VID as one of ours, so it neither
+ * takes the tag off the frame nor reads the chip port out of it, and the
+ * frame leaves the chip aimed at the fallback port with the tag still on
+ * it.  The 6.18 generation has this range at 59..63 and its 2.5G jack
+ * works (docs/UPSTREAM-2G5-LANE-FIX section 12.7).
+ */
+#define ZX279133_LAN_TRANSPORT_VID_MAX	63
 #define ZX279133_LAN_INGRESS_VID	1
 #define ZX279133_LAN_VID		62
 #define ZX279133_LAN_MAX_MTU		(ZX279133_MAX_MTU + VLAN_HLEN)
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-np.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-np.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-np.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-np.c
@@ -1402,7 +1402,30 @@
 	{ 0x1c8c2800, 0x2fc36000, 0x00000a60, 0 },	/* VID 60, lan3 */
 	{ 0x1c8c2800, 0x30636a00, 0x00000a88, 0 },	/* VID 61, lan2 */
 	{ 0x1c8c2800, 0x31037400, 0x00000ab0, 0 },	/* VID 62, lan1 */
+	/*
+	 * VID 63 is the 2.5G jack's, and this entry is the one number
+	 * in this file that is not a measurement.  The factory system has
+	 * four LAN ports and no fifth, so there is nothing on this board
+	 * to read the fifth entry from.  What the four measured entries
+	 * do is hand out the per-port areas in a run: word 1 steps by
+	 * 0x00a00a00 and word 2 by 0x28 from one port to the next, and
+	 * word 0 - the port attribute word - is the same for all four.
+	 * This entry continues both runs, which is what the factory
+	 * system would have written had it had a fifth port.  It is the
+	 * same entry, word for word, that the 6.18 generation carries.
+	 */
+	{ 0x1c8c2800, 0x31a37e00, 0x00000ad8, 0 },	/* VID 63, the 2.5G jack */
 };
+
+/*
+ * The table is indexed by the transport VID, and a logical port that
+ * has no entry here reads past the end of the array and writes
+ * whatever follows it into the switch's table.  The fifth user port
+ * came with the fifth VID and the entry had to come with it; this
+ * makes the second one impossible to forget.
+ */
+static_assert(ARRAY_SIZE(zx279133_lan_vport_mf) ==
+	      ZX279133_LAN_VPORT_LAST - ZX279133_LAN_VPORT_FIRST + 1);
 
 /* SDT 6: one entry per logical port; bit 31 of word 3 marks a LAN port. */
 #define ZX279133_PORT_TYPE_LAN			BIT(31)
